's another power efficiency victory  for Maxwell and a win for MSI's cooler  connectivity wise there's just a single  eight pin power connector to hook up  along with one dual link DVI output one  HDMI and three full-sized display ports  all gtx 960 support the upcoming direct  x12 along with in videos full suite of  game works which you can learn about in  our gtx 980 review right here so the gtx  960 s got the features but how does it  perform hooked up to our core i7 3770k  system with sixteen gigabytes of ddr3  ram the card breezed through most all  are 1080p benchmarks running our test  suite of games at above sixty frames per  second with all the settings cranked  naturally the only game not to do so was  perennial system breaker Crysis 3 which  managed a playable but not entirely  smooth 40 frames per second average as  for higher resolutions the gtx 960 isn't  the best choice particularly as it only  has 2 gigabytes of memory but there are  playable frame rates that hover above 30  frames per second to be had at 1440p if  your entire gaming life revolves around  stuff like dota League of Legends or  counter-strike then naturally 1440p are  well above 60 frames per second is no  problem for this card performance is  good then but not earth-shattering in  the same way as the GTX 970  that said it is noticeably faster than  the outgoing GTX 760 and uses less power  and if you're running an older 660 or  560 it's certainly worth the upgrade  AMD's offerings at the same price are  very competitive though that's  especially true of the r9 280 which  retails for the same $200 that actually  features a 384 bit memory interface and  three gigabytes of memory yes it's based  on an old architecture but it stands  toe-to-toe with the gtx 960 on  performance and the extra memory is  great for those games with large  textures or if you're into modern games  like Skyrim so the gtx 960 might not be  the revolution in value that the GTX 970  was and the two gigabytes of memory is  stingy by today's standards but there's  still no touching in video when it comes  to power efficiency resulting in the  coolest quietest cards around if you're  after a capable 1080p GPU it's a good  choice for the cache  but with GPU memory requirements on the  rise I'm keen to see what AMD can come  up with later this year
 

